{
  // Every leaf in the house: what actually swings, slides, tilts or lifts in each portal.
  // `HOUSE-00378`. `layout.portals.json` says where the hole is; this says what fills it, and
  // §15.7 rule 7 is the bijection between the two -- every door and window has exactly one
  // portal, every leaf-bearing portal names its opening in `aperture`, and no portal carries two.
  //
  // Sizes are §12.3's and §12.6's schedules and are DERIVED from the portal rather than repeated
  // beside it: a leaf is the rough opening less its reveal, 0.04 m across and 0.05 m up for a
  // door, 0.02 m all round for a window sash. Nothing here can disagree with the hole it hangs
  // in, and moving a portal's rect without touching this file is caught by arithmetic and not by
  // eye. The two rows that are stated rather than derived say so in their type comment.
  //
  // `swing` names the cell the leaf opens INTO: the smaller of the two, because a door swings
  // into the room it serves and not into the corridor; never the outdoors, whatever the areas
  // say, because a leaf opening over a porch step would foul it; and never down a stair, because
  // a door at the head of a flight must not swing over the top step. `hinge` is the side with
  // more wall beside it, so the leaf folds flat against something instead of standing in the
  // middle of the room. Both are derived and both are stated: they are design decisions, and a
  // hundred and thirty chosen by hand would be a hundred and thirty chances to be arbitrary in a
  // different way.
  //
  // `asset` and `frame.asset` are null throughout. They are model references and the models are
  // phase 6 work; a made-up id here would point at nothing and rule 6 could not tell. `material`
  // is NOT null: those ten ids are an obligation on `HOUSE-00385`, which authors
  // `layout.materials.json`, and rule 6 holds it to them the moment that file exists.
  "schema": "cna-house/openings/1",
